{"name":"Toreador","hex":"#cd123f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"toreador","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A bullfighter, especially one on horseback."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/toreador"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bullfighter","blocks":[{"text":"A bullfighter or matador is a performer in the activity of bullfighting. Torero or toureiro, both from Latin taurarius, are the Spanish and Portuguese words for bullfighter, and describe all the performers in the activity of bullfighting as practised in Spain, Portugal, Mexico, Peru, France, Colombia, Ecuador, Venezuela and other countries influenced by Portuguese and Spanish culture. The main performer and leader of the entourage in a bullfight, and who finally kills the bull, is addressed as maestro (master), or with the formal title matador de toros. The other bullfighters in the entourage are called subalternos and their suits are embroidered in silver as opposed to the matador's gold. They include the picadores, rejoneadores, and banderilleros."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Corrida%2C_Rio_Lagartos%2C_Yucatan.jpg/330px-Corrida%2C_Rio_Lagartos%2C_Yucatan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bullfighter"},{"title":"The Toreador","blocks":[{"text":"The Toreador is an Edwardian musical comedy in two acts by James T. Tanner and Harry Nicholls, with lyrics by Adrian Ross and Percy Greenbank and music by Ivan Caryll and Lionel Monckton. It opened at the Gaiety Theatre in London, managed by George Edwardes, on 17 June 1901 and ran for an extremely successful 675 performances. It starred Marie Studholme, Gertie Millar, Harry Grattan, Edmund Payne, George Grossmith, Jr. and the young Sidney Bracy. Gabrielle Ray later joined the cast. The show also enjoyed Broadway runs in 1902 and 1904 and toured internationally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/The_Toreador.jpg/330px-The_Toreador.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Toreador"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg/500px-10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg/1280px-10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg/500px-10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A10_-_Cristina_S%C3%A1nchez.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/MatadorEarly20thCentury.jpg/500px-MatadorEarly20thCentury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/MatadorEarly20thCentury.jpg/500px-MatadorEarly20thCentury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Early 20th-century photograph of a matador, showing traditional outfit in 1935.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMatadorEarly20thCentury.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Joselito_y_Belmonte.png/500px-Joselito_y_Belmonte.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Joselito_y_Belmonte.png/500px-Joselito_y_Belmonte.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Joselito and Belmonte","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJoselito_y_Belmonte.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/